{"units": [{"id": "anamplaketos-def1", "translation": "free from error, who has done no wrong"}, {"id": "anamplaketos-note1", "translation": "Similarly Soph. Tr. 120 ἀλλά τις θεῶν αἰὲν ἀναμπλάκητον Ἅιδα σφε δόμων ἐρύκει [but some god always keeps him from the house of Hades, free from error]. Thus Camerarius for ἀπλάκητον [unblameable], which Hesychius also reads, ἀπλάκητον: ἀναμάρτητον [unblameable: without sin]. Σ. Τραχινίαις [Scholia on the Trachiniae]."}, {"id": "anamplaketos-quote1", "translation": "If the army comes to the gods free from error, it may turn back the other leg of the double course."}, {"id": "anamplaketos-quote2", "translation": "But some god always keeps him from the house of Hades, free from error."}, {"id": "anamplaketos-quote3", "translation": "unblameable: without sin. Scholia on the Trachiniae."}, {"id": "anamplaketos-word1", "translation": "unblameable"}, {"id": "anamychthizomai-orth1", "translation": "to groan aloud"}, {"id": "anamychthizomai-def1", "translation": "I groan aloud"}, {"id": "anamychthizomai-quote1", "translation": "But you in turn have cried out and groan aloud (κἀναμυχθίζῃ [you groan aloud] M)."}, {"id": "anandria-orth1", "translation": "cowardice"}, {"id": "anandria-def1", "translation": "cowardice"}, {"id": "anandria-quote1", "translation": "because of cowardice"}, {"id": "anandros-orth1", "translation": "bereft of men, lacking men"}, {"id": "anandros-def1", "translation": "bereft of men, lacking men"}, {"id": "anandros-note1", "translation": "(i.e. of men absent from home) πλῆθος [the multitude]."}, {"id": "anandros-note2", "translation": "where ἄνανδρον [manless] is said of what follows from the battle line being deserted by the dying, as Hermann notes, who says that the proleptic use of adjectives is frequent. Similarly Sophocles OR. 58 πόλις ἔρημος ἀνδρῶν μὴ ξυνοικούντων ἔσω [a city empty of men not dwelling within]."}, {"id": "anandros-quote1", "translation": "of manless wealth"}, {"id": "anandros-quote2", "translation": "the multitude."}, {"id": "anandros-quote3", "translation": "They made many Persian women vainly bereft of husbands and manless."}, {"id": "anandros-quote4", "translation": "having died, he left the battle line manless and desolate,"}, {"id": "anandros-quote5", "translation": "a city empty of men not dwelling within."}, {"id": "anandros-quote6", "translation": "the manless and flesh-eating Amazons."}, {"id": "anandros-word1", "translation": "manless"}, {"id": "anax-orth1", "translation": "king"}, {"id": "anax-def1", "translation": "king"}, {"id": "anax-def2", "translation": "king, leader"}, {"id": "anax-note1", "translation": "Of the gods"}, {"id": "anax-note2", "translation": "(Jupiter)."}, {"id": "anax-note3", "translation": "(Apollo)."}, {"id": "anax-note4", "translation": "Of men,"}, {"id": "anax-note5", "translation": "[But there at line 651 ἄνακτα [lord] must be changed to ἀνάκτορα [palace], as is said under ἀνάκτωρ [lord].]"}, {"id": "anax-note6", "translation": "where the sense requires ἀεὶ γυναικῶν [always of women], which Linwood conjectured."}, {"id": "anax-note7", "translation": "Of him who steers a ship"}, {"id": "anax-quote1", "translation": "Do not begrudge me my prayers, lord"}, {"id": "anax-quote2", "translation": "sea-ruling lord Poseidon."}, {"id": "anax-quote3", "translation": "lord Apollo."}, {"id": "anax-quote4", "translation": "of lord Helios."}, {"id": "anax-quote5", "translation": "Zeus the lord."}, {"id": "anax-quote6", "translation": "the Pythian lord"}, {"id": "anax-quote7", "translation": "lord Apollo,"}, {"id": "anax-quote8", "translation": "lord Apollo."}, {"id": "anax-quote9", "translation": "lord of lords — blessed Zeus."}, {"id": "anax-quote10", "translation": "the lord with his own hand - Zeus."}, {"id": "anax-quote11", "translation": "Zeus the lord."}, {"id": "anax-quote12", "translation": "and the common altar of all these lords."}, {"id": "anax-quote13", "translation": "lord of the Cadmeans."}, {"id": "anax-quote14", "translation": "this lord himself, the offspring of Oedipus."}, {"id": "anax-quote15", "translation": "lord of evils."}, {"id": "anax-quote16", "translation": "lord Xerxes."}, {"id": "anax-quote17", "translation": "and to the lord himself,"}, {"id": "anax-quote18", "translation": "we hear that the lord escaped on foot."}, {"id": "anax-quote19", "translation": "lord Darius."}, {"id": "anax-quote20", "translation": "I heard of this lord."}, {"id": "anax-quote21", "translation": "lord Seualces."}, {"id": "anax-quote22", "translation": "the hand of the lord of the house."}, {"id": "anax-quote23", "translation": "lord Menelaus."}, {"id": "anax-quote24", "translation": "and the lord, the elder."}, {"id": "anax-quote25", "translation": "lord Agamemnon."}, {"id": "anax-quote26", "translation": "lord son of Atreus."}, {"id": "anax-quote27", "translation": "of the lord himself."}, {"id": "anax-quote28", "translation": "O lord."}, {"id": "anax-quote29", "translation": "and in the house, with these gods, lord, it is yours to have."}, {"id": "anax-quote30", "translation": "you dared to bury the lord without the citizens."}, {"id": "anax-quote31", "translation": "and the Delphian, the lord of this land, the helmsman."}, {"id": "anax-quote32", "translation": "and of my lord."}, {"id": "anax-quote33", "translation": "lord of the Pelasgians."}, {"id": "anax-quote34", "translation": "lord of the Pelasgians."}, {"id": "anax-quote35", "translation": "lord, give the order."}, {"id": "anax-quote36", "translation": "unexpected things, lord, we suffer."}, {"id": "anax-quote37", "translation": "of these two lords."}, {"id": "anax-quote38", "translation": "and always there is an extraordinary fear of lords,"}, {"id": "anax-quote39", "translation": "you will see many lords."}, {"id": "anax-quote40", "translation": "every man is lord of the oar."}, {"id": "anax-quote41", "translation": "lords of ships."}, {"id": "anax-quote42", "translation": "mistress nymph, lord of the wintry mountains."}, {"id": "anax-word1", "translation": "lord"}, {"id": "anax-word2", "translation": "palace"}, {"id": "anax-word3", "translation": "lord"}, {"id": "anax-word4", "translation": "always of women"}, {"id": "anaxia-orth1", "translation": "kingdom"}, {"id": "anaxia-def1", "translation": "kingdom"}, {"id": "anaxia-note1", "translation": ", which Hesychius explains by βασιλείαν [kingship]."}, {"id": "anaxia-word1", "translation": "kingship"}, {"id": "anaxia_see-orth1", "translation": "unworthy"}, {"id": "anaxia_see-note1", "translation": "[erroneously for ἄξια [worthy]. See ἄξιος [worthy].]"}, {"id": "anaxia_see-word1", "translation": "worthy"}, {"id": "anaxia_see-word2", "translation": "worthy"}, {"id": "anapauo-orth1", "translation": "to make rest or cease"}, {"id": "anapauo-def1", "translation": "to make rest or cease"}, {"id": "anapauo-quote1", "translation": "he rests the immortal skin and the toil of the horses."}, {"id": "anapempo-orth1", "translation": "to send up"}, {"id": "anapempo-def1", "translation": "I send up"}, {"id": "anapempo-quote1", "translation": "sending up from below the late-punishing ruin."}, {"id": "anapipto_see-orth1", "translation": "to fall back"}, {"id": "anapipto_see-word1", "translation": "to fall back"}, {"id": "anapompos-orth1", "translation": "sending up"}, {"id": "anapompos-def1", "translation": "sending up"}, {"id": "anapompos-quote1", "translation": "and Hades (or Aidoneus) sends up the divine lord of grief, Darius."}, {"id": "anapteroo-orth1", "translation": "to furnish with wings"}, {"id": "anapteroo-def1", "translation": "I furnish with wings"}, {"id": "anapteroo-note1", "translation": "Improperly of one who is raised in hope"}, {"id": "anapteroo-quote1", "translation": "you were winged with excitement and thought you saw me."}, {"id": "anaptysso-orth1", "translation": "to unfold"}, {"id": "anaptysso-def1", "translation": "I unfold"}, {"id": "anaptysso-quote1", "translation": "to unfold the whole suffering."}]}